Let $\phi: G \to A$ be a [[Definition:Group Homomorphism|homomorphism]] where $A$ is [[Definition:Abelian Group|abelian]].
Let $x, y \in G$.
Then:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = \map \phi {x y x^{-1} y^{-1} }
| r = \map \phi x \map \phi y \map \phi x^{-1} \map \phi y^{-1}
| c = {{Defof|Group Homomorphism}}
}}
{{eqn | r = e_A
| c = as $A$ is [[Definition:Abelian Group|abelian]]
}}
{{end-eqn}}
Hence:
:$\sqbrk {G, G} \subseteq \map \ker \phi$

As $q$ is a [[Definition:Surjection|surjection]], any $\psi$ with $\psi \circ q = \phi$ agrees with $\bar \phi$ on every [[Definition:Coset|coset]].
Hence $\bar \phi$ is [[Definition:Unique|unique]].
{{qed}}
